00:04:57.490 --> 00:05:00.880
Deep time and intelligence.

00:05:00.880 --> 00:05:04.120
If there were a single thread
that I felt knit together

00:05:04.120 --> 00:05:07.360
these three fascinating
talks on sound, time,

00:05:07.360 --> 00:05:09.010
and the formation
of intelligence

00:05:09.010 --> 00:05:11.470
across scales and
modes of existence,

00:05:11.470 --> 00:05:15.460
I would have to hone in
on the concept of variety.

00:05:15.460 --> 00:05:18.550
The question of variation is
a fascinating one, clearly

00:05:18.550 --> 00:05:21.940
central to Gary's attempt
to define radical niche

00:05:21.940 --> 00:05:26.650
construction by sensitizing
us to the pervasive plasticity

00:05:26.650 --> 00:05:28.420
of niche construction.

00:05:28.420 --> 00:05:30.340
In that context
of inquiry, I was

00:05:30.340 --> 00:05:34.240
able to sense the core of
the key semiotic information

00:05:34.240 --> 00:05:36.760
concept when Gary
spoke of a bird

00:05:36.760 --> 00:05:41.020
listening and singing as the
varied complexity of the world

00:05:41.020 --> 00:05:43.450
proportionate to the
varied complexity

00:05:43.450 --> 00:05:47.140
of the brain architectures,
thereby enlivened.

00:05:47.140 --> 00:05:50.620
To react, to reach after
clarity with respects

00:05:50.620 --> 00:05:53.950
to a genuinely
semiotic variety here

00:05:53.950 --> 00:05:56.110
would have to enliven
a sense in us,

00:05:56.110 --> 00:05:58.930
not only of the emergent
selection and optimization

00:05:58.930 --> 00:06:03.940
of a function, say, for
addition or vocalization,

00:06:03.940 --> 00:06:06.280
or for auditory
feature detection,

00:06:06.280 --> 00:06:09.640
but a sense of the plasticity
of niche constructions

00:06:09.640 --> 00:06:11.980
that take place
through a mind that can

00:06:11.980 --> 00:06:17.650
relive the relation of interior
variety to exterior variety.

00:06:17.650 --> 00:06:21.340
The sound of bird sing
in this light, sounds

00:06:21.340 --> 00:06:23.050
by which a bird does
not merely fulfill

00:06:23.050 --> 00:06:26.050
a reproductive function, but
rather relives a relation

00:06:26.050 --> 00:06:30.910
to not only another bird, but
to the variety of song, senses

00:06:30.910 --> 00:06:33.490
and throws a voice
from that vocal commons

00:06:33.490 --> 00:06:34.870
in a new direction.

00:06:34.870 --> 00:06:37.690
Recast the tone of
another, let's say,

00:06:37.690 --> 00:06:42.730
play with, in, and through
the plasticity of the niche.

00:06:42.730 --> 00:06:45.130
Learning to sense that
plasticity, to respect it,

00:06:45.130 --> 00:06:47.950
a feat of reflection,
surely, and one

00:06:47.950 --> 00:06:50.860
I believe the question
of AI training practices

00:06:50.860 --> 00:06:52.750
can help us think about.

00:06:52.750 --> 00:06:56.830
I would be curious to hear
Gary's thoughts and thoughts

00:06:56.830 --> 00:07:00.400
on his talk that would help us
listen for the hints of when it

00:07:00.400 --> 00:07:03.490
is that AI forces us
into thinking clearly

00:07:03.490 --> 00:07:06.010
about the genuinely meaningful.

00:07:06.010 --> 00:07:10.120
Does AI open up such a space
of reflection on variation?

00:07:10.120 --> 00:07:13.270
Or does it lock us into a
thought of the functional?

00:07:13.270 --> 00:07:15.970
Perhaps before we
ask, is AI as smart

00:07:15.970 --> 00:07:19.690
as a bee, I want to hear
in Gary's final provocation

00:07:19.690 --> 00:07:23.740
a questioning that enlists
us, asking, have we,

00:07:23.740 --> 00:07:26.920
in entertaining Gary's
reanimation of the mental world

00:07:26.920 --> 00:07:29.230
of birds and bees,
found our thoughts

00:07:29.230 --> 00:07:32.560
wandering to a thought
or relation to AI that

00:07:32.560 --> 00:07:34.900
sees the maintenance
of a differentiation

00:07:34.900 --> 00:07:38.250
of the functional from
the meaningful at work?

00:07:38.250 --> 00:07:42.060
Curious, then, to listen
to Holly and Markus.

00:07:42.060 --> 00:07:44.370
Fascinating that both
of you also named

00:07:44.370 --> 00:07:48.210
Bach when discussing this
concern over variation.

00:07:48.210 --> 00:07:51.390
One of the central concerns
in training AI systems to sing

00:07:51.390 --> 00:07:56.110
and play is that they not
reproduce in AI systems

00:07:56.110 --> 00:07:59.310
aesthetic cul-de-sacs,
as Holly put it,

00:07:59.310 --> 00:08:02.010
but how practically
to avoid that.

00:08:02.010 --> 00:08:04.590
I know that Markus is
very interested in using

00:08:04.590 --> 00:08:08.610
MIDI data to train his AI, and
that Holly and Markus both have

00:08:08.610 --> 00:08:12.000
different approaches to
using either MIDI or audio

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:14.460
data in their training sets.

00:08:14.460 --> 00:08:17.520
So I was also just wondering
simply if Antonio had thoughts

00:08:17.520 --> 00:08:20.820
on generating and designing
audio training sets

00:08:20.820 --> 00:08:23.580
and what stakes that
technical decision

00:08:23.580 --> 00:08:27.960
has in preserving or shaping
data into something that

00:08:27.960 --> 00:08:30.180
generates desirable results.

00:08:30.180 --> 00:08:33.480
What is a desirable
result, then, a surprise?

00:08:33.480 --> 00:08:35.640
An optimal performance?

00:08:35.640 --> 00:08:38.309
A novel or meaningful variation?

00:08:38.309 --> 00:08:41.340
Taking this thread of
data generation back

00:08:41.340 --> 00:08:44.340
to radical plasticity with
Holly's differentiation

00:08:44.340 --> 00:08:48.660
of sampling from spawning, I
heard an important distinction

00:08:48.660 --> 00:08:50.400
being drawn, one
that would force

00:08:50.400 --> 00:08:52.710
us to rethink the
plasticity of the niche

00:08:52.710 --> 00:08:56.730
in the economic context of
the digital music marketplace.

00:08:56.730 --> 00:08:59.730
I was inspired by Holly's
phrase when she pointed out

00:08:59.730 --> 00:09:02.550
that we must remain
attuned to the entitlement

00:09:02.550 --> 00:09:06.420
to a shared musical past
when building data sets.

00:09:06.420 --> 00:09:09.390
Would it be generative to
invoke the concept of niche

00:09:09.390 --> 00:09:13.110
and then infuse the
plasticity of same with stakes

00:09:13.110 --> 00:09:16.290
cultural, political,
and ethico-aesthetic?

00:09:16.290 --> 00:09:19.590
Does AI help us think about
the mutable plasticity

00:09:19.590 --> 00:09:22.080
of the sonic commons,
in other words?

00:09:22.080 --> 00:09:24.630
Scene ownership and
niche production

00:09:24.630 --> 00:09:28.500
seem to complement and
trouble each other here.

00:09:28.500 --> 00:09:30.780
And I would love to
hear further thoughts

00:09:30.780 --> 00:09:34.860
in the vein of this role of
AI in shaping a reflection on

00:09:34.860 --> 00:09:38.730
or even staging a variation
in scene construction

00:09:38.730 --> 00:09:40.800
or constriction.

00:09:40.800 --> 00:09:43.500
The idea of variation for Markus
seemed to hinge on the idea

00:09:43.500 --> 00:09:45.750
that AI can add new information.

00:09:45.750 --> 00:09:49.260
In his case, working with
molecular dynamics simulation,

00:09:49.260 --> 00:09:50.880
that information
would be generated

00:09:50.880 --> 00:09:53.400
by computers trained
on the archaic memory

00:09:53.400 --> 00:09:57.090
of primordial forces at
work in protein folding.

00:09:57.090 --> 00:10:00.030
Those forces adding
to and reconfiguring

00:10:00.030 --> 00:10:03.570
data presented to our machines
from the musical archive.

00:10:03.570 --> 00:10:06.240
Here, then, data
set creation comes

00:10:06.240 --> 00:10:08.490
into dialogue with
data generation

00:10:08.490 --> 00:10:10.710
and also points to the
mathematical and scientific

00:10:10.710 --> 00:10:14.400
constraints placed on
information generation.

00:10:14.400 --> 00:10:16.500
Professor Torralba,
too, in his comments

00:10:16.500 --> 00:10:19.380
on the manual manipulation
of GAN-generated images

00:10:19.380 --> 00:10:21.270
touches on the
concept of variation

00:10:21.270 --> 00:10:24.750
when he suggests that neural
nets have imaginations.

00:10:24.750 --> 00:10:26.760
In the history of
philosophy and poetry,

00:10:26.760 --> 00:10:29.130
the variation of images
in the imagination

00:10:29.130 --> 00:10:31.800
was famously called upon by
Locke and Hobbes and Hume,

00:10:31.800 --> 00:10:36.480
Blake and Coleridge, and
made a point of contention.

00:10:36.480 --> 00:10:39.150
Are those variations
always concatenations

00:10:39.150 --> 00:10:43.230
of sensory impressions,
primary and secondary qualities

00:10:43.230 --> 00:10:46.020
or properties of matter
reconfigured by the brain?

00:10:46.020 --> 00:10:49.590
Or are their forms derived
from the mind or spirit

00:10:49.590 --> 00:10:53.880
that intervene and shape that
raw material of variation?

00:10:53.880 --> 00:10:56.460
With the manual addition
of grass neurons

00:10:56.460 --> 00:10:59.430
to dome-drawing neurons,
those questions somehow

00:10:59.430 --> 00:11:02.130
become more
concrete, less binary

00:11:02.130 --> 00:11:05.370
in the hylomorphism
of mind and matter.

00:11:05.370 --> 00:11:08.490
While we could localize
the function grass,

00:11:08.490 --> 00:11:10.650
even in a single
artificial neuron,

00:11:10.650 --> 00:11:12.630
those localized
manipulations produce

00:11:12.630 --> 00:11:14.760
results that seem
to imply a network

00:11:14.760 --> 00:11:17.550
of other related
functions, like on top,

00:11:17.550 --> 00:11:20.430
yellowish, fuzzy, gleaming.

00:11:20.430 --> 00:11:23.670
In seeing these
instances of localization

00:11:23.670 --> 00:11:26.430
in neurons returning
weird results, condensing

00:11:26.430 --> 00:11:29.430
so many categories into
a pixel distribution,

00:11:29.430 --> 00:11:32.580
I was wondering if Markus or
Gary saw something to trouble

00:11:32.580 --> 00:11:36.150
or confirm their thoughts on
properties versus processes,

00:11:36.150 --> 00:11:39.570
patterns of energy
localization versus plasticity

00:11:39.570 --> 00:11:40.860
set in motion.

00:11:40.860 --> 00:11:44.310
Are neural nets unhinging
us from the object,

00:11:44.310 --> 00:11:46.590
from stable
substrates configured

00:11:46.590 --> 00:11:49.560
into materials with properties?

00:11:49.560 --> 00:11:53.400
And certainly I can return
to some of those questions

00:11:53.400 --> 00:11:55.140
and call on individuals.

00:11:55.140 --> 00:11:57.780
But I will perhaps
leave the floor

00:11:57.780 --> 00:12:01.545
open to see what reactions
this might have produced.

00:12:14.590 --> 00:12:17.270
Holly, I see you smiling.

00:12:17.270 --> 00:12:20.220
Would you care to interject?

00:12:20.220 --> 00:12:20.720
Sure.

00:12:20.720 --> 00:12:22.400
Thanks so much
for that overview.

00:12:22.400 --> 00:12:26.030
And thanks to all the
panelists for your really

00:12:26.030 --> 00:12:27.980
interesting and
inspiring presentations.

00:12:27.980 --> 00:12:31.550
I really enjoyed getting to
learn about all of your work.

00:12:31.550 --> 00:12:34.100
Maybe I could just touch
on some of the things

00:12:34.100 --> 00:12:36.737
that you brought up.

00:12:36.737 --> 00:12:38.570
I did kind of chuckle
to myself a little bit

00:12:38.570 --> 00:12:40.940
when I was watching
Markus's presentation

00:12:40.940 --> 00:12:42.830
and he brought up Bach.

00:12:42.830 --> 00:12:44.550
Because in my
presentation, of course,

00:12:44.550 --> 00:12:47.460
I'm using it as a
criticism, as you mentioned

00:12:47.460 --> 00:12:50.510
this kind of aesthetic
cul-de-sac, this idea

00:12:50.510 --> 00:12:52.400
that a lot of machine
learning research

00:12:52.400 --> 00:12:55.910
is looking to kind of
recreate forms of the past.

00:12:55.910 --> 00:12:58.160
But what I found interesting
about Markus's work

00:12:58.160 --> 00:13:01.460
is that he's actually
bringing in other forms

00:13:01.460 --> 00:13:05.570
through the protein
structures in order

00:13:05.570 --> 00:13:10.610
to kind of open up and add
new information to the Bach

00:13:10.610 --> 00:13:11.370
training set.

00:13:11.370 --> 00:13:14.293
So I think he avoids
the aesthetic cul-de-sac

00:13:14.293 --> 00:13:15.460
in a really interesting way.

00:13:19.430 --> 00:13:20.108
Yeah.

00:13:20.108 --> 00:13:21.650
Maybe I can add to
this a little bit.

00:13:21.650 --> 00:13:23.240
I mean, Will, thanks for
the great introduction,

00:13:23.240 --> 00:13:24.440
and I took some notes.

00:13:24.440 --> 00:13:26.750
And there's a lot of really,
really cool intersections

00:13:26.750 --> 00:13:27.950
that you identified.

00:13:27.950 --> 00:13:30.840
But, yeah I wanted to say
that what we do in the work,

00:13:30.840 --> 00:13:34.520
we're trying to put different
levels of information

00:13:34.520 --> 00:13:38.270
from different species or
different scales on a platform

00:13:38.270 --> 00:13:39.530
where they can communicate.

00:13:39.530 --> 00:13:41.960
And we call it sometimes
interspecies communications

00:13:41.960 --> 00:13:44.120
when we talk about
spiders speaking

00:13:44.120 --> 00:13:47.228
with humans or, in
this case, for Bach,

00:13:47.228 --> 00:13:49.520
looking at data that's been
generated hundreds of years

00:13:49.520 --> 00:13:52.670
ago, speaking with data that's
been generated over billions

00:13:52.670 --> 00:13:54.080
of years during evolution.

00:13:54.080 --> 00:13:55.820
And we found that
that transition

00:13:55.820 --> 00:13:57.980
into an abstraction,
whether be it

00:13:57.980 --> 00:14:00.200
mathematically rigorous
like in category theory,

00:14:00.200 --> 00:14:04.670
or using sound or coding into
these audible barcodes, the way

00:14:04.670 --> 00:14:05.280
to do that.

00:14:05.280 --> 00:14:06.905
And what's interesting
is that then you

00:14:06.905 --> 00:14:09.020
have the ability to
use the AI or computers

00:14:09.020 --> 00:14:12.108
as a way of creating this
communication in a way that

00:14:12.108 --> 00:14:13.400
wouldn't be possible otherwise.

00:14:13.400 --> 00:14:17.720
Because either the
actors are long dead,

00:14:17.720 --> 00:14:19.790
or they cannot
talk to each other.

00:14:19.790 --> 00:14:21.950
Like we cannot speak
to spiders immediately.

00:14:21.950 --> 00:14:23.810
So I found sound to be
a very effective way

00:14:23.810 --> 00:14:25.100
for this abstraction.

00:14:25.100 --> 00:14:27.830
And adding new
information is something

00:14:27.830 --> 00:14:30.200
I would say we have done for
many hundreds or thousands

00:14:30.200 --> 00:14:31.310
of years, actually.

00:14:31.310 --> 00:14:33.750
But computers allow us to
do this in a perhaps more

00:14:33.750 --> 00:14:36.650
systematic way, or reaching
observational levels

00:14:36.650 --> 00:14:38.180
that our brain isn't wired for.

00:14:38.180 --> 00:14:40.220
So I found it really
interesting when

00:14:40.220 --> 00:14:42.380
Antonio was talking
about finding

00:14:42.380 --> 00:14:46.200
the neurons in particular layers
that [INAUDIBLE] in an image

00:14:46.200 --> 00:14:47.700
or create dome structures.

00:14:47.700 --> 00:14:50.900
And I think that's a really
interesting mechanistic insight

00:14:50.900 --> 00:14:52.580
we can gain from
these neural nets

00:14:52.580 --> 00:14:55.010
that it's really hard to
pinpoint in our own brain

00:14:55.010 --> 00:14:57.590
why we can identify these kinds
of things in our own brain

00:14:57.590 --> 00:15:00.780
very easily.

00:15:00.780 --> 00:15:01.980
Yes.

00:15:01.980 --> 00:15:03.590
I can comment a
little bit on that.

00:15:03.590 --> 00:15:06.690
So I think that it's
very interesting

00:15:06.690 --> 00:15:11.670
to see what these AI systems are
learning when you present them

00:15:11.670 --> 00:15:13.050
with training data.

00:15:13.050 --> 00:15:15.870
And it is true that
there is a piece of it

00:15:15.870 --> 00:15:20.570
that is about recreating the
past, as Holly was mentioning.

00:15:20.570 --> 00:15:24.840
But in this representation that
the systems are discovering,

00:15:24.840 --> 00:15:27.210
which are not for humans.

00:15:27.210 --> 00:15:30.840
Humans are only presenting
the data to the systems.

00:15:30.840 --> 00:15:33.760
But the representation seems
to be somewhat meaningful.

00:15:33.760 --> 00:15:35.250
And this is what
allows us, then,

00:15:35.250 --> 00:15:38.850
to manipulate the representation
and to put grass in domes

00:15:38.850 --> 00:15:39.910
and so on.

00:15:39.910 --> 00:15:43.230
And one thing that we
discovered that it's not

00:15:43.230 --> 00:15:45.600
just that there are
units that represent

00:15:45.600 --> 00:15:50.490
particular concepts that
emerge just from the training

00:15:50.490 --> 00:15:53.940
procedure itself, but
also these relationships.

00:15:53.940 --> 00:15:57.060
As it was mentioned
in the introduction,

00:15:57.060 --> 00:15:59.130
the fact that grass
only grows on top

00:15:59.130 --> 00:16:01.770
of certain surfaces,
that was something

00:16:01.770 --> 00:16:04.080
that at the beginning, it
just seemed to us as being

00:16:04.080 --> 00:16:05.820
quite accidental.

00:16:05.820 --> 00:16:10.080
But then we discovered
that actually the network

00:16:10.080 --> 00:16:12.090
was learning all
those relationships.

00:16:12.090 --> 00:16:14.100
For instance, there
are some units

00:16:14.100 --> 00:16:17.880
that will learn to draw doors.

00:16:17.880 --> 00:16:20.740
But now you can
manipulate them and say

00:16:20.740 --> 00:16:22.230
where you want to have a door.

00:16:22.230 --> 00:16:24.060
But you cannot put
a door anywhere.

00:16:24.060 --> 00:16:25.710
Like if you have
an image and you

00:16:25.710 --> 00:16:28.020
try to put a door on
the sky, the network

00:16:28.020 --> 00:16:29.730
will just not do it.

00:16:29.730 --> 00:16:34.530
Even though you are activating
the units that draw doors,

00:16:34.530 --> 00:16:36.390
those activations
will not survive

00:16:36.390 --> 00:16:40.440
the rest of the process, and
they will get canceled out.

00:16:40.440 --> 00:16:43.500
And the network learns all
these kinds of relationships.

00:16:43.500 --> 00:16:45.360
And one interesting
thing is that now you

00:16:45.360 --> 00:16:49.020
can imagine a process
where you change

00:16:49.020 --> 00:16:53.220
the way that the
network generates data

00:16:53.220 --> 00:16:55.950
so that it violates some
of these principles,

00:16:55.950 --> 00:17:01.500
and it starts generating data
that looks somewhat real,

00:17:01.500 --> 00:17:05.560
but it is something that
does not necessarily exist.

00:17:05.560 --> 00:17:08.790
You can start putting things
where it's not possible.

00:17:08.790 --> 00:17:12.750
You can change the laws
of light transmission.

00:17:12.750 --> 00:17:15.390
For instance, one
thing that we noticed

00:17:15.390 --> 00:17:17.520
is that whenever
there is a window,

00:17:17.520 --> 00:17:19.260
the system learns
that there should

00:17:19.260 --> 00:17:23.730
be some reflections appearing
on top of shiny surfaces.

00:17:23.730 --> 00:17:26.550
Well, you can change those rules
and start having reflections

00:17:26.550 --> 00:17:27.900
where there are no windows.

00:17:27.900 --> 00:17:30.750
Or reflections disappear
when there are windows.

00:17:30.750 --> 00:17:35.320
And there are very simple
changes that you need to make.

00:17:35.320 --> 00:17:37.590
So one could imagine
that eventually, you

00:17:37.590 --> 00:17:42.330
could generate data that feels
plausible but is not real

00:17:42.330 --> 00:17:44.670
and breaking that
cul-de-sac that Holly

00:17:44.670 --> 00:17:45.930
was mentioning at some point.

00:17:50.680 --> 00:17:52.360
May I leap in as well here?

00:17:52.360 --> 00:17:54.340
The presentations
were all fascinating.

00:17:54.340 --> 00:17:57.520
Will, thank you so much for
the wonderful introduction.

00:17:57.520 --> 00:18:02.770
The question of grass
on the top of the church

00:18:02.770 --> 00:18:04.280
is what I want to return to.

00:18:04.280 --> 00:18:08.250
And Antonio, I
thought the machine

00:18:08.250 --> 00:18:10.750
learning that was going on there
was absolutely fascinating.

00:18:10.750 --> 00:18:14.530
I really wanted to press you
to understand exactly what it

00:18:14.530 --> 00:18:18.070
means to think that there is
a single neuron, so to speak,

00:18:18.070 --> 00:18:20.190
that is the grass
neuron, for example,

00:18:20.190 --> 00:18:21.700
that has learned somehow.

00:18:21.700 --> 00:18:25.330
Because, of course, attuned
neurons in biological systems

00:18:25.330 --> 00:18:26.500
are absolutely fundamental.

00:18:26.500 --> 00:18:29.500
The honeybee sucrose
neuron, for example,

00:18:29.500 --> 00:18:31.570
that I mentioned
in my presentation

00:18:31.570 --> 00:18:32.720
is an example of that.

00:18:32.720 --> 00:18:34.720
And the sensitivity
of it is constantly

00:18:34.720 --> 00:18:39.100
in plastic change in
relation to its environment.

00:18:39.100 --> 00:18:42.280
But one of the things that is
striking about those images

00:18:42.280 --> 00:18:44.620
that were generated by--
machine-generated images

00:18:44.620 --> 00:18:47.470
after this learning
process came up

00:18:47.470 --> 00:18:49.690
in the question of the
watermark that you mentioned.

00:18:49.690 --> 00:18:52.060
It is to me exactly the
mark of the difference

00:18:52.060 --> 00:18:56.560
between a machine algorithm
that has learned certain things

00:18:56.560 --> 00:18:58.450
but doesn't know
how to contextualize

00:18:58.450 --> 00:19:02.680
this learning in an environment
and a biological mind.

00:19:02.680 --> 00:19:05.920
The difference is precisely in
the fact that no biological--

00:19:05.920 --> 00:19:08.110
no human taking all
of those pictures

00:19:08.110 --> 00:19:10.000
and instructed to
draw another picture

00:19:10.000 --> 00:19:11.260
would include the watermarks.

00:19:11.260 --> 00:19:14.440
And yet the machine cannot but
include the watermarks unless

00:19:14.440 --> 00:19:16.612
instructed to do differently.

00:19:16.612 --> 00:19:17.320
Where would you--

00:19:20.470 --> 00:19:22.060
Yeah.

00:19:22.060 --> 00:19:23.560
That's an interesting question.

00:19:23.560 --> 00:19:27.250
The machine realizes--
well, realizes.

00:19:27.250 --> 00:19:30.940
It tries to reproduce the same
data that you give it to read.

00:19:30.940 --> 00:19:33.040
And watermarks are part of it.

00:19:33.040 --> 00:19:36.340
Watermarks were not present
in all of the images,

00:19:36.340 --> 00:19:38.870
but there is a percentage of
them that contain watermarks.

00:19:38.870 --> 00:19:40.510
So what happened
is that the network

00:19:40.510 --> 00:19:44.700
will produce, sometimes,
images with watermarks.

00:19:44.700 --> 00:19:48.450
And in fact, we located where
those watermarks are generated.

00:19:48.450 --> 00:19:50.550
And it's not just
one single unit.

00:19:50.550 --> 00:19:53.070
Generally what you have is
a small population of units,

00:19:53.070 --> 00:19:59.130
maybe 20 units out of 500, that
represent a particular object.

00:19:59.130 --> 00:20:01.050
And in particular
watermarks, there

00:20:01.050 --> 00:20:03.630
are some units that were
doing those watermarks.

00:20:03.630 --> 00:20:06.780
So now you can,
for instance, say,

00:20:06.780 --> 00:20:09.910
I want these units
to never be active.

00:20:09.910 --> 00:20:12.430
And from that moment,
all of the images

00:20:12.430 --> 00:20:16.023
that this process will generate
will not contain watermarks.

00:20:16.023 --> 00:20:18.190
Or you could reverse it and
always have them active,

00:20:18.190 --> 00:20:20.620
and then all of the images
will have a watermark.

00:20:20.620 --> 00:20:27.610
So the system doesn't have the
knowledge of what this means.

00:20:27.610 --> 00:20:30.700
So for a human, you could know
that the watermark is really

00:20:30.700 --> 00:20:34.210
not important and then
decide not to put it ever.

00:20:34.210 --> 00:20:37.750
But the machine, the
watermark or a door,

00:20:37.750 --> 00:20:39.220
they are all equally important.

00:20:41.980 --> 00:20:42.480
So--

00:20:42.480 --> 00:20:45.890
I could actually
imagine-- sorry?

00:20:45.890 --> 00:20:46.390
Please.

00:20:46.390 --> 00:20:48.598
I was just thinking specifically
about the watermark.

00:20:48.598 --> 00:20:51.940
It reminds me of kind of
[INAUDIBLE] expressions, where

00:20:51.940 --> 00:20:53.650
artists began to
incorporate some

00:20:53.650 --> 00:20:57.250
of these kind of aesthetics
into their own aesthetic.

00:20:57.250 --> 00:21:02.500
So I could actually imagine
a kind of human creative mind

00:21:02.500 --> 00:21:04.113
also integrating the watermark.

00:21:04.113 --> 00:21:05.530
But then that
would, of course, be

00:21:05.530 --> 00:21:09.490
through a kind of human
understanding of comedy,

00:21:09.490 --> 00:21:13.200
or the context would
be entirely different.

00:21:13.200 --> 00:21:14.120
Yeah.

00:21:14.120 --> 00:21:16.820
And in a certain
sense, the context--

00:21:16.820 --> 00:21:19.940
the word "imagination" came
up at the end in regard

00:21:19.940 --> 00:21:23.060
to the grass on the
top of the church.

00:21:23.060 --> 00:21:26.180
The human imagination--
talking about deep time

00:21:26.180 --> 00:21:28.100
and intelligence, the
human imagination--

00:21:28.100 --> 00:21:31.310
there's a wonderful phrase
that an archeologist introduced

00:21:31.310 --> 00:21:34.040
30 years ago and
that has been tracked

00:21:34.040 --> 00:21:38.240
in archeological proxies
across 500,000 years of hominin

00:21:38.240 --> 00:21:40.850
evolution, long before
Homo sapiens came along.

00:21:40.850 --> 00:21:43.250
And the phrase is the
release from proximity.

00:21:43.250 --> 00:21:47.960
That is, what gradually unfolded
in hominin intelligence was

00:21:47.960 --> 00:21:51.650
the capacity to imagine things
that weren't there, that

00:21:51.650 --> 00:21:55.250
weren't in front of the senses,
in some cases imagined things

00:21:55.250 --> 00:21:57.920
that were minimal
counterintuitions as opposed

00:21:57.920 --> 00:22:00.060
to what was actually
seen in the environment.

00:22:00.060 --> 00:22:03.320
In more dramatic cases, finally
to imagine things that never

00:22:03.320 --> 00:22:05.210
have existed.

00:22:05.210 --> 00:22:08.660
And of course, humans are
fantastically good at this.

00:22:08.660 --> 00:22:13.250
This release from proximity is
a long-term evolutionary thing

00:22:13.250 --> 00:22:15.350
that hominins have
come to be able to do.

00:22:15.350 --> 00:22:17.990
Neanderthals were probably
pretty good at it too.

00:22:17.990 --> 00:22:22.120
But certainly Homo sapiens, all
Homo sapiens, are great at it.

00:22:22.120 --> 00:22:26.750
That seems to be different from
simply manifesting something

00:22:26.750 --> 00:22:28.520
that never existed
through the machine

00:22:28.520 --> 00:22:30.080
learning that you're
talking about.

00:22:30.080 --> 00:22:32.360
And I think those
differences-- this is not

00:22:32.360 --> 00:22:34.828
to underplay the
extraordinary achievement

00:22:34.828 --> 00:22:36.620
of these neural nets
that you put together.

00:22:36.620 --> 00:22:37.953
This is amazing, it seems to me.

00:22:37.953 --> 00:22:39.320
It's amazing work.

00:22:39.320 --> 00:22:41.330
And yet the gap
remains, somehow,

00:22:41.330 --> 00:22:43.490
between what they're
up to and what

00:22:43.490 --> 00:22:47.910
a human imagination, any human
imagination, can be up to.

00:22:47.910 --> 00:22:51.240
Maybe I can share a
few thoughts on this.

00:22:51.240 --> 00:22:53.670
[INAUDIBLE] This is a
brilliant, brilliant point.

00:22:53.670 --> 00:22:57.030
Thank you, Gary, and also,
really, to the other ones.

00:22:57.030 --> 00:23:01.440
What we've been
doing with using data

00:23:01.440 --> 00:23:03.870
from original
compositions like Bach

00:23:03.870 --> 00:23:06.210
was to actually seek
information that might

00:23:06.210 --> 00:23:07.620
have been encoded in this data.

00:23:07.620 --> 00:23:08.700
So we used MIDI data.

00:23:08.700 --> 00:23:10.830
That's another discussion
on whether MIDI data

00:23:10.830 --> 00:23:13.360
versus audio data or
fluctuations of that.

00:23:13.360 --> 00:23:17.010
But let's say we take that data,
and we've been seeking patterns

00:23:17.010 --> 00:23:19.680
within that data that
holds hidden messages

00:23:19.680 --> 00:23:22.320
that the creator of this
data, Bach in this case,

00:23:22.320 --> 00:23:24.810
or other people that might
have created music at the time,

00:23:24.810 --> 00:23:28.240
have encoded things in that
data they weren't aware of.

00:23:28.240 --> 00:23:31.230
So I'd like to hear, actually,
Gary's thoughts on this

00:23:31.230 --> 00:23:35.436
on what the context might be
from your perspective on this.

00:23:35.436 --> 00:23:37.703
So it's another level
of human imagination,

00:23:37.703 --> 00:23:39.120
not just things
that aren't there,

00:23:39.120 --> 00:23:41.880
but imagining things
unconsciously that were there

00:23:41.880 --> 00:23:44.000
in the past but that
have been forgotten.

00:23:44.000 --> 00:23:46.050
So these structures
of our protein folding

00:23:46.050 --> 00:23:47.790
or internal structures
in the organs

00:23:47.790 --> 00:23:50.460
weren't known at the time
when Bach created the music.

00:23:50.460 --> 00:23:52.350
Yet we've shown that
there are structures

00:23:52.350 --> 00:23:54.540
in the music he
created that resembled

00:23:54.540 --> 00:23:57.292
biological systems that
have been evolving over

00:23:57.292 --> 00:23:59.250
billions of years in the
evolution of proteins,

00:23:59.250 --> 00:24:01.860
for instance.

00:24:01.860 --> 00:24:04.195
I don't have-- of
course, Markus,

00:24:04.195 --> 00:24:07.110
I don't have any
full answer to such

00:24:07.110 --> 00:24:09.840
a broad and wonderful
question and the kinds

00:24:09.840 --> 00:24:15.270
of questions that are raised
by what you've been working on.

00:24:15.270 --> 00:24:21.510
And yet, what you are
demonstrating, it seems to me,

00:24:21.510 --> 00:24:33.390
is the continuity across the
cross even cosmic dimensions

00:24:33.390 --> 00:24:36.960
and at all scales, spatial
and temporal scales,

00:24:36.960 --> 00:24:39.930
of vibrational essences.

00:24:39.930 --> 00:24:42.990
Those vibrational
essences are in themselves

00:24:42.990 --> 00:24:46.350
manifesting at least a
rudimentary structure.

00:24:46.350 --> 00:24:48.330
The notion of vibration
is a structure,

00:24:48.330 --> 00:24:51.390
it seems to me, a
process at the same time,

00:24:51.390 --> 00:24:53.650
and a dynamic structure,
but a structure.

00:24:53.650 --> 00:24:58.950
And so to begin to unfold
the continuity across all

00:24:58.950 --> 00:25:04.920
those degrees of scale, the
continuity between that kind

00:25:04.920 --> 00:25:11.460
of structure and the structures
that human minds come up with.

00:25:11.460 --> 00:25:13.590
Will mentioned in his
introduction birdsong.

00:25:13.590 --> 00:25:16.290
And of course I mentioned
in my talk about birdsong.

00:25:16.290 --> 00:25:19.920
I am constantly wanting
to say that of course we

00:25:19.920 --> 00:25:20.820
call it birdsong.

00:25:20.820 --> 00:25:23.460
Of course we humans do all
kinds of things with this.

00:25:23.460 --> 00:25:28.170
But to call it song at all
is probably a misnomer,

00:25:28.170 --> 00:25:32.040
as long as we are imposing
some general sense

00:25:32.040 --> 00:25:35.970
of the human value in song
onto those sonic structures.

00:25:35.970 --> 00:25:38.340
But they are immensely
complex sonic structures,

00:25:38.340 --> 00:25:40.320
immensely communicative
sonic structures

00:25:40.320 --> 00:25:42.120
in complex social systems.

00:25:42.120 --> 00:25:46.710
And all of it, then,
comes back to the sense--

00:25:46.710 --> 00:25:50.160
well, the vibrational sense
that you are working with.

00:25:50.160 --> 00:25:52.080
So my question for
you, Markus, would

00:25:52.080 --> 00:25:54.440
be once you have
translated, for example,

00:25:54.440 --> 00:25:56.730
the aria to the
Goldberg Variations,

00:25:56.730 --> 00:25:59.370
once you've translated
it through the filter

00:25:59.370 --> 00:26:06.000
of amino acid vibrational
dynamics, well, two things.

00:26:06.000 --> 00:26:10.260
It doesn't come back as the
aria that Bach composed,

00:26:10.260 --> 00:26:12.600
even though we could
hear traces of that aria.

00:26:12.600 --> 00:26:14.670
We could hear echoes
here and there.

00:26:14.670 --> 00:26:16.922
It comes back as something
very, very different.

00:26:16.922 --> 00:26:18.630
But why doesn't it
come back as something

00:26:18.630 --> 00:26:20.820
even further, even
more different, even

00:26:20.820 --> 00:26:21.960
more radically different?

00:26:21.960 --> 00:26:27.150
Haven't you converted some
of these amino acid dynamics

00:26:27.150 --> 00:26:30.390
into a set of pictures that
is, after all, a very, very

00:26:30.390 --> 00:26:32.844
small subset of all the
frequencies that we hear?

00:26:32.844 --> 00:26:33.344
Right.

00:26:33.344 --> 00:26:34.080
Yeah.

00:26:34.080 --> 00:26:34.580
Correct.

00:26:34.580 --> 00:26:37.100
I think maybe in
responding to that,

00:26:37.100 --> 00:26:43.130
I'm curious to reconfigure
the question slightly,

00:26:43.130 --> 00:26:47.330
to maybe bring us back to a
question that's coming through

00:26:47.330 --> 00:26:49.340
in the backchannel
chats right now,

00:26:49.340 --> 00:26:55.100
is that when we're having these
discussions about whether it is

00:26:55.100 --> 00:26:57.680
merely a function or
some kind of meaning,

00:26:57.680 --> 00:27:02.090
or whether it is a genuine
variation or something that

00:27:02.090 --> 00:27:05.030
repeats a difference that's
already encoded in the data

00:27:05.030 --> 00:27:08.720
set, I'm curious if, as
practicing scientists

00:27:08.720 --> 00:27:12.170
and artists, if you
find yourself developing

00:27:12.170 --> 00:27:15.260
an intuitive sense
of how to negotiate

00:27:15.260 --> 00:27:17.840
those questions in the
choices that you're

00:27:17.840 --> 00:27:19.900
making as practitioners.

00:27:19.900 --> 00:27:24.740
So when you're searching
for a genuine variation,

00:27:24.740 --> 00:27:28.160
what kinds of techniques
or choices are you making?

00:27:28.160 --> 00:27:33.380
When are you confronted with
those almost ontological

00:27:33.380 --> 00:27:36.840
conundrums through the work
that you're generating?

00:27:36.840 --> 00:27:40.970
And I think that that has to
do with the aesthetic choices

00:27:40.970 --> 00:27:43.160
that maybe Markus
would be confronted

00:27:43.160 --> 00:27:46.070
with in seeing this new
variation of the aria

00:27:46.070 --> 00:27:51.020
and having to decide whether
to perhaps change the force

00:27:51.020 --> 00:27:54.260
equation that he's using
to model the protein fold.

00:27:54.260 --> 00:27:58.550
Or for Holly, in building
these file systems

00:27:58.550 --> 00:27:59.930
of all these
different variations

00:27:59.930 --> 00:28:02.240
that you showed us
on these audio files,

00:28:02.240 --> 00:28:05.780
negotiating somehow with
what the basic building

00:28:05.780 --> 00:28:09.080
blocks of a composition
are as they're

00:28:09.080 --> 00:28:11.540
derived from this engagement
with machine learning systems.

00:28:11.540 --> 00:28:12.770
So yeah, I'm really curious.

00:28:12.770 --> 00:28:14.540
And Gary's question,
too, but I think

00:28:14.540 --> 00:28:18.230
that that dimension of
practice is something

00:28:18.230 --> 00:28:20.780
that the audience is curious
in hearing more about.

00:28:30.530 --> 00:28:34.080
That was directed,
I had understood,

00:28:34.080 --> 00:28:37.065
particularly at Markus I don't
want to put him on the spot.

00:28:37.065 --> 00:28:37.955
Or Holly.

00:28:37.955 --> 00:28:38.810
[INAUDIBLE]

00:28:38.810 --> 00:28:41.060
Or professor Torralba is
also engaged in the practice

00:28:41.060 --> 00:28:42.450
of building data sets as well.

00:28:42.450 --> 00:28:45.786
So I'm curious if that--

00:28:48.734 --> 00:28:51.425
Well, maybe I can
briefly answer and then

00:28:51.425 --> 00:28:52.800
everyone else also
say something.

00:28:52.800 --> 00:28:55.700
But there are a lot of
choices, of course, we make.

00:28:55.700 --> 00:28:57.560
And I would say a little
bit to the question

00:28:57.560 --> 00:29:01.130
of Gary and the generalization
that Will talked about,

00:29:01.130 --> 00:29:03.770
we choose, of course, the
universe or the platform we're

00:29:03.770 --> 00:29:04.310
building.

00:29:04.310 --> 00:29:06.482
Let's say the sounds
from-- and in this case,

00:29:06.482 --> 00:29:07.940
we're working with
the amino acids.

00:29:07.940 --> 00:29:12.110
And actually in the case of
the deep area we created,

00:29:12.110 --> 00:29:15.320
we're actually working not in
the innate amino acid scale,

00:29:15.320 --> 00:29:18.770
but in an equal
temperament scaling

00:29:18.770 --> 00:29:21.610
to reflect back what Bach
actually originally intended

00:29:21.610 --> 00:29:22.610
the music to sound like.

00:29:22.610 --> 00:29:24.140
So we make a lot
of choices in this,

00:29:24.140 --> 00:29:27.140
and it's a discussion
that's much longer in what

00:29:27.140 --> 00:29:30.190
are the more abstract
ways to look at that.

00:29:30.190 --> 00:29:31.940
And I think your
birdsong example actually

00:29:31.940 --> 00:29:33.020
reminded me of that.

00:29:33.020 --> 00:29:35.540
When we do listen to
proteins or structures

00:29:35.540 --> 00:29:39.080
like this in the innate
vibrational spectrum,

00:29:39.080 --> 00:29:40.698
we have a hard time
understanding them

00:29:40.698 --> 00:29:42.740
and accessing them in our
brain because we're not

00:29:42.740 --> 00:29:46.140
attuned to these ways of
thinking or listening.

00:29:46.140 --> 00:29:48.110
So we are trying to make
choices that make it

00:29:48.110 --> 00:29:50.150
accessible to certain regions.

00:29:50.150 --> 00:29:52.640
I would say that we want
to make interpolations

00:29:52.640 --> 00:29:55.340
similar to an expansion in the
series, a tailor expansion,

00:29:55.340 --> 00:29:57.290
where we want to look
at a reference point,

00:29:57.290 --> 00:30:00.380
in this case the aria, and
the original way it's played

00:30:00.380 --> 00:30:03.530
in equal temperament and
then extrapolate from there

00:30:03.530 --> 00:30:06.290
a little bit out, what would
this additional information

00:30:06.290 --> 00:30:09.770
from protein folding
add to the structure?

00:30:09.770 --> 00:30:11.270
So we can make those
choices, and we

00:30:11.270 --> 00:30:14.660
can go either very far away, or
we can stay reasonably close.

00:30:14.660 --> 00:30:18.560
In this piece that I
present in the presentation,

00:30:18.560 --> 00:30:21.433
it was a compromise
between going too far,

00:30:21.433 --> 00:30:22.850
but also being
able, like you say,

00:30:22.850 --> 00:30:24.230
to recognize fragments of it.

00:30:24.230 --> 00:30:26.990
And that's what I wanted to show
is there are these fragments.

00:30:26.990 --> 00:30:28.500
You can recognize them.

00:30:28.500 --> 00:30:30.500
But there are also
differences and distinctions,

00:30:30.500 --> 00:30:31.958
which are very
clear, and they tell

00:30:31.958 --> 00:30:34.940
the story of what this deep
time memory really brings to us.

00:30:41.253 --> 00:30:42.920
So it's not the
computer alone doing it.

00:30:42.920 --> 00:30:47.030
I think Will had that
point when we discussed,

00:30:47.030 --> 00:30:50.300
and I also mentioned
this in my talk.

00:30:50.300 --> 00:30:53.550
In the rendering of the
actual final product,

00:30:53.550 --> 00:30:56.060
there are many choices
we make as humans.

00:30:56.060 --> 00:30:57.680
And that's oftentimes
misunderstood.

00:30:57.680 --> 00:31:01.730
You have these algorithms
that create songs.

00:31:01.730 --> 00:31:04.730
And I think Holly mentioned
some of those things in her talk

00:31:04.730 --> 00:31:05.970
as well.

00:31:05.970 --> 00:31:08.910
There are a lot of choices, of
course, that we need to make.

00:31:08.910 --> 00:31:11.810
And we're very far away from--
and maybe perhaps infinitely

00:31:11.810 --> 00:31:13.970
far away from letting it
be completely automated

00:31:13.970 --> 00:31:15.614
by an algorithm.

00:31:19.880 --> 00:31:22.280
For me as a composer,
a practitioner,

00:31:22.280 --> 00:31:25.497
I my ultimate goal is
I'm trying to create

00:31:25.497 --> 00:31:27.330
meaning through the
artwork that I'm making.

00:31:27.330 --> 00:31:30.800
So it's a kind of
meaning-making search.

00:31:30.800 --> 00:31:33.560
And so, as Markus
mentioned, you can't really

00:31:33.560 --> 00:31:36.560
outsource that to a machine.

00:31:36.560 --> 00:31:38.900
Really, I see machine
learning techniques

00:31:38.900 --> 00:31:44.690
as a way to augment my
process, particularly, ideally,

00:31:44.690 --> 00:31:47.150
an embodied
performance practice.

00:31:47.150 --> 00:31:49.730
That's where I really think
things will get interesting

00:31:49.730 --> 00:31:51.200
and start to take off.

00:31:51.200 --> 00:31:53.870
When we're able to,
as humans, music

00:31:53.870 --> 00:31:56.690
with other humans and machines
in kind of concert together,

00:31:56.690 --> 00:32:00.140
that's when I think new
aesthetic possibilities

00:32:00.140 --> 00:32:01.540
and new ideas will emerge.

00:32:01.540 --> 00:32:04.430
And so that's what I'm
primarily focusing on right now.

00:32:04.430 --> 00:32:07.430
I haven't been focusing
so much on kind

00:32:07.430 --> 00:32:11.210
of the statistical analysis
of certain existing forms

00:32:11.210 --> 00:32:14.490
and trying to kind of
create new forms from that.

00:32:14.490 --> 00:32:16.910
So that's another
reason why I really

00:32:16.910 --> 00:32:22.010
tried to focus on using audio
material rather than MIDI data,

00:32:22.010 --> 00:32:24.890
because as a sound
artist and composer,

00:32:24.890 --> 00:32:31.940
I find so many kind of sensual
qualities in sound as material.

00:32:31.940 --> 00:32:36.590
And for me, a kind of MIDI
representation of a piece

00:32:36.590 --> 00:32:39.080
is missing out on so
many of the things

00:32:39.080 --> 00:32:42.020
that I find sensual about music.

00:32:42.020 --> 00:32:44.450
So trying to kind
of understand what

00:32:44.450 --> 00:32:46.790
might be sensual
about one sound source

00:32:46.790 --> 00:32:49.880
and then trying to
understand how I might then

00:32:49.880 --> 00:32:52.430
augment my own performance
practice to be able to perform

00:32:52.430 --> 00:32:54.200
through that sound.

00:32:54.200 --> 00:32:59.150
That's been kind of my approach,
to remain in the driver's seat

00:32:59.150 --> 00:33:02.510
of composer and meaning maker.

00:33:06.220 --> 00:33:07.930
Yeah, I think there
is also something

00:33:07.930 --> 00:33:11.530
interesting with respect to
the choices that we make.

00:33:11.530 --> 00:33:14.380
There are choices at so
many levels, the data

00:33:14.380 --> 00:33:18.500
that we use to train our system,
the representation that we use,

00:33:18.500 --> 00:33:21.490
whether it's MIDI
or the real audio,

00:33:21.490 --> 00:33:25.420
and also the architecture that
we use to process that data.

00:33:25.420 --> 00:33:29.590
And the choices that we don't
make, we still make them.

00:33:29.590 --> 00:33:31.240
We are just not aware of them.

00:33:31.240 --> 00:33:36.490
So that's where all the
biases come in that creep

00:33:36.490 --> 00:33:38.200
into our systems.

00:33:38.200 --> 00:33:39.980
And we might not
be aware of them,

00:33:39.980 --> 00:33:42.970
but the system is just trying
to reproduce what it sees.

00:33:42.970 --> 00:33:44.560
And it's limited
by the architecture

00:33:44.560 --> 00:33:47.080
and the representational
power that it has,

00:33:47.080 --> 00:33:49.390
given the choices that you
made on how you implement

00:33:49.390 --> 00:33:51.500
that particular approach.

00:33:51.500 --> 00:33:54.420
So all of these
things have an impact

00:33:54.420 --> 00:33:59.235
on the results that you have
and the behavior that you have

00:33:59.235 --> 00:34:00.610
when you use a
particular system.

00:34:04.830 --> 00:34:07.390
A phrase of yours,
Antonio, that came up

00:34:07.390 --> 00:34:11.440
in your earlier comments,
"not all of the variations

00:34:11.440 --> 00:34:14.244
survived the rest
of the process."

00:34:14.244 --> 00:34:17.170
It was very fascinating to
me that you could manually

00:34:17.170 --> 00:34:19.810
manipulate with
something in the system

00:34:19.810 --> 00:34:22.840
that you're using to
generate a variation,

00:34:22.840 --> 00:34:27.219
but that the system seems to
have implicit constraints built

00:34:27.219 --> 00:34:28.449
into it.

00:34:28.449 --> 00:34:32.290
That is something
that also reminded me

00:34:32.290 --> 00:34:35.560
of Markus's approach
in the sense

00:34:35.560 --> 00:34:40.030
that there are limitations
or constraints on the kinds

00:34:40.030 --> 00:34:42.670
of data that he can
generate, and that

00:34:42.670 --> 00:34:45.280
has an interesting creative
effect on the work,

00:34:45.280 --> 00:34:48.159
that working within
limitations is one

00:34:48.159 --> 00:34:51.730
of the interesting
aspects of the new kinds

00:34:51.730 --> 00:34:54.070
of creative processes
that are at work here.

00:34:54.070 --> 00:34:57.790
It's almost that we know about
rule-constrained art making

00:34:57.790 --> 00:35:00.160
practices from conceptual
art, for example.

00:35:00.160 --> 00:35:04.660
But we're operating with a
different set of limitations

00:35:04.660 --> 00:35:08.350
here, ones that we may not even
be able to explicitly identify

00:35:08.350 --> 00:35:10.360
if all of the rules
governing the net

00:35:10.360 --> 00:35:12.700
are not completely
transparent to us.

00:35:12.700 --> 00:35:17.170
So I'm curious if these
thoughts on the kinds

00:35:17.170 --> 00:35:25.150
of limitation-setting
role of AI have been--

00:35:25.150 --> 00:35:29.030
whether those have been of
interest to you as researchers

00:35:29.030 --> 00:35:31.030
and whether you
wonder about what

00:35:31.030 --> 00:35:33.340
constrains the
validity of results

00:35:33.340 --> 00:35:34.900
in these sorts of
systems, whether it

00:35:34.900 --> 00:35:37.990
be scientifically
or aesthetically.

00:35:37.990 --> 00:35:42.850
Yeah, I think that the
way that I see current AI

00:35:42.850 --> 00:35:46.510
systems, especially the
ones using neural networks,

00:35:46.510 --> 00:35:48.940
I think it's been true
maybe for a long time,

00:35:48.940 --> 00:35:51.940
but especially true
nowadays is that we

00:35:51.940 --> 00:35:53.770
have these systems
that are composed

00:35:53.770 --> 00:35:56.050
of very simple operations.

00:35:56.050 --> 00:35:59.340
And they have to, through
these simple operations

00:35:59.340 --> 00:36:01.780
and recombining them,
they have to produce

00:36:01.780 --> 00:36:03.310
some complicated task.

00:36:03.310 --> 00:36:05.200
They have to solve
some complicated task.

00:36:05.200 --> 00:36:08.200
And at each iteration
of the process,

00:36:08.200 --> 00:36:11.320
I see it as a filter
in a stage, where

00:36:11.320 --> 00:36:15.220
you remove undesired
sources of variation,

00:36:15.220 --> 00:36:17.290
and a recombination
a stage, where

00:36:17.290 --> 00:36:20.410
you try to find new
concepts from the data

00:36:20.410 --> 00:36:22.630
or build a more
sophisticated representation.

00:36:22.630 --> 00:36:25.250
But that recombination
process is very simple.

00:36:25.250 --> 00:36:28.150
So the way that you build
a complex representation is

00:36:28.150 --> 00:36:31.360
by removing undesired
sources of variation

00:36:31.360 --> 00:36:34.690
and recombining the concepts a
little bit and then removing,

00:36:34.690 --> 00:36:37.960
again, undesired sources of
variation in this new space

00:36:37.960 --> 00:36:40.450
and recombining
again, and so on.

00:36:40.450 --> 00:36:42.250
And that's how the
process evolves.

00:36:42.250 --> 00:36:46.000
And this is why you need
a lot of different stages

00:36:46.000 --> 00:36:46.780
of processing.

00:36:46.780 --> 00:36:48.160
And in the case
of the brain, you

00:36:48.160 --> 00:36:52.300
have all the different
layers of neurons

00:36:52.300 --> 00:36:54.820
that process the
data just moving

00:36:54.820 --> 00:36:57.188
one step towards
whatever task you

00:36:57.188 --> 00:36:59.230
want to solve at the end,
whatever representation

00:36:59.230 --> 00:37:00.520
you want to build.

00:37:00.520 --> 00:37:05.080
And I think that we are
very interested in trying

00:37:05.080 --> 00:37:08.742
to see how much of
that representation

00:37:08.742 --> 00:37:10.450
and the way that that
representation will

00:37:10.450 --> 00:37:13.090
get transformed is
understandable by us.

00:37:13.090 --> 00:37:15.640
And I think that some part
of it will be understandable,

00:37:15.640 --> 00:37:17.098
and there are some
other the pieces

00:37:17.098 --> 00:37:19.810
that will not,
that maybe are not

00:37:19.810 --> 00:37:24.340
because they are not really
based on rules, necessarily,

00:37:24.340 --> 00:37:27.160
but are a little
bit more intuitive.

00:37:27.160 --> 00:37:29.200
But I think that that
is a big piece of it

00:37:29.200 --> 00:37:31.840
that will be understandable.

00:37:31.840 --> 00:37:33.760
That's my personal belief.

00:37:33.760 --> 00:37:35.925
And from the artistic
point of view,

00:37:35.925 --> 00:37:37.300
I think that is
very interesting.

00:37:37.300 --> 00:37:39.467
Then, once you have this
representation of something

00:37:39.467 --> 00:37:42.550
and you understand some of the
rules, how do you break them?

00:37:42.550 --> 00:37:45.110
And what are the things
that you get out?

00:37:45.110 --> 00:37:47.020
And I think that things
like abstract art

00:37:47.020 --> 00:37:51.240
and so on are maybe
representations that preserve

00:37:51.240 --> 00:37:53.980
rules to some level
of that processing,

00:37:53.980 --> 00:37:55.600
but they don't go any farther.

00:37:55.600 --> 00:37:58.870
You don't try to recombine
those things together

00:37:58.870 --> 00:38:00.910
at the end like real objects.

00:38:00.910 --> 00:38:04.210
You are just stopping
very early on

00:38:04.210 --> 00:38:06.580
and try to see, OK, how
does the representation

00:38:06.580 --> 00:38:07.740
look like at this level?

00:38:13.090 --> 00:38:17.630
I want to leap in here and
push a little bit further

00:38:17.630 --> 00:38:19.070
on that word "representation."

00:38:19.070 --> 00:38:21.840
It's a word that is used by
cognitivists all the time.

00:38:21.840 --> 00:38:23.600
It's one of those
words that I have

00:38:23.600 --> 00:38:26.450
trouble with because it's
rarely defined as clearly

00:38:26.450 --> 00:38:27.080
as it might be.

00:38:27.080 --> 00:38:28.760
A mental
representation-- this is

00:38:28.760 --> 00:38:32.330
a phrase that recurs over
the last 40 years everywhere.

00:38:32.330 --> 00:38:35.510
And we still have very little--

00:38:35.510 --> 00:38:38.870
well, we have ideas about what a
mental representation might be.

00:38:38.870 --> 00:38:41.840
And indeed, it comes down
to something that looks--

00:38:41.840 --> 00:38:43.310
it seems to come
down to something

00:38:43.310 --> 00:38:46.100
that looks, Antonio, a little
bit like what it is coming down

00:38:46.100 --> 00:38:50.270
to precisely in the
algorithmic neural nets that

00:38:50.270 --> 00:38:54.290
are learning in your work.

00:38:54.290 --> 00:38:58.160
But, boy, here's where
my imagination fails me.

00:38:58.160 --> 00:39:03.590
To get from there to what it is
that allows me to put together

00:39:03.590 --> 00:39:06.380
a whole elaborate
context that tells me,

00:39:06.380 --> 00:39:10.130
in trying to draw a picture
that answers to 50 pictures

00:39:10.130 --> 00:39:12.470
that you've shown me, that
I don't need to include

00:39:12.470 --> 00:39:13.970
the watermark, for example.

00:39:13.970 --> 00:39:17.270
That, it seems to
me, is a huge leap

00:39:17.270 --> 00:39:21.080
beyond what machine learning
is doing to this point.

00:39:21.080 --> 00:39:25.400
And Will focused, in his
remarks on my presentation,

00:39:25.400 --> 00:39:28.610
focused on the
question of plasticity

00:39:28.610 --> 00:39:31.803
and the relationship between
organism and environment.

00:39:31.803 --> 00:39:33.720
Genes are not what we
once thought them to be,

00:39:33.720 --> 00:39:34.340
it turns out.

00:39:34.340 --> 00:39:37.610
Genes are not blueprints.

00:39:37.610 --> 00:39:41.120
They are, in fact,
interacting at all moments

00:39:41.120 --> 00:39:44.090
with the environment
on all scales, down

00:39:44.090 --> 00:39:46.700
to the momentary scale, in
fact, in terms of which genes

00:39:46.700 --> 00:39:50.480
can be expressed at a
moment's notice in order

00:39:50.480 --> 00:39:52.020
to direct behavior.

00:39:52.020 --> 00:39:53.960
So they have a directive
executive function.

00:39:53.960 --> 00:39:56.520
And yet that function is
nothing like a stable blueprint,

00:39:56.520 --> 00:39:57.750
nothing like it at all.

00:39:57.750 --> 00:40:00.110
So in that sense,
intelligence itself

00:40:00.110 --> 00:40:03.200
can't be localized in either
a machine or in my brain.

00:40:03.200 --> 00:40:06.530
Intelligence is localized
only in the process

00:40:06.530 --> 00:40:11.510
that extends from my brain
out to the whole environment

00:40:11.510 --> 00:40:13.740
or from the machine out
to the whole environment.

00:40:13.740 --> 00:40:15.260
And so the gist
of my talk finally

00:40:15.260 --> 00:40:19.580
was to ask whether machine
learning is coming anywhere

00:40:19.580 --> 00:40:26.000
close to that kind of
hugely varied on all scales

00:40:26.000 --> 00:40:28.370
interrelation and hugely
complex interrelation

00:40:28.370 --> 00:40:32.690
between environment and machine.

00:40:40.270 --> 00:40:41.840
Well, let me say one thing.

00:40:41.840 --> 00:40:48.160
But I think that one missing
piece is the embodiment.

00:40:48.160 --> 00:40:50.980
So current AI systems
are not embodied

00:40:50.980 --> 00:40:52.990
or are poorly embodied.

00:40:52.990 --> 00:40:56.080
And I think that is--

00:40:56.080 --> 00:40:57.880
the case of the watermark.

00:40:57.880 --> 00:40:59.950
Why are watermarks important?

00:40:59.950 --> 00:41:03.280
It is really because
many other things that

00:41:03.280 --> 00:41:06.070
are outside of the picture.

00:41:06.070 --> 00:41:09.170
There are other
social implications

00:41:09.170 --> 00:41:13.520
that go outside of what
the picture contains.

00:41:13.520 --> 00:41:17.120
And AI systems,
generally they focus

00:41:17.120 --> 00:41:19.310
on few sensory modalities.

00:41:19.310 --> 00:41:21.320
Now that we've been
talking about audio.

00:41:21.320 --> 00:41:22.770
You have images.

00:41:22.770 --> 00:41:25.100
There is also the tactile world.

00:41:25.100 --> 00:41:27.350
But it's when you put
all these things together

00:41:27.350 --> 00:41:30.260
that you start grounding things.

00:41:30.260 --> 00:41:33.380
And you still have
these limitations.

00:41:33.380 --> 00:41:36.290
Even when you have multiple
sensory modalities,

00:41:36.290 --> 00:41:40.640
you will still have some
other choices to make.

00:41:40.640 --> 00:41:42.890
But I think that it provides
some additional grounding

00:41:42.890 --> 00:41:46.730
that AI systems now
don't really have.

00:41:46.730 --> 00:41:51.230
It's not that the research
community is not aware of that.

00:41:51.230 --> 00:41:52.340
They are aware of it.

00:41:52.340 --> 00:41:55.000
It's just that it takes
time to get there.

00:41:55.000 --> 00:41:58.580
And I think many of the
advances that we are seeing now,

00:41:58.580 --> 00:42:02.510
they have taken us by
surprise in the last decade.

00:42:02.510 --> 00:42:05.300
And it takes a little
bit more time than that

00:42:05.300 --> 00:42:07.370
to start building other things.

00:42:07.370 --> 00:42:09.950
And there are still
many other breakthroughs

00:42:09.950 --> 00:42:11.960
that are necessary.

00:42:11.960 --> 00:42:16.100
Really all of this is
just now just learning

00:42:16.100 --> 00:42:18.080
relationships between things.

00:42:18.080 --> 00:42:21.650
And going through
a machine that is

00:42:21.650 --> 00:42:23.990
able to have critical
thinking and things like that

00:42:23.990 --> 00:42:28.520
is something much
more complicated.

00:42:28.520 --> 00:42:31.430
Can I piggyback off of
that wonderful response

00:42:31.430 --> 00:42:34.760
and ask Holly a question?

00:42:34.760 --> 00:42:39.110
Holly, one of the last things
you left us with was the effort

00:42:39.110 --> 00:42:44.840
to have spawn work in your
performances in real time,

00:42:44.840 --> 00:42:48.270
in your live performances
in real time.

00:42:48.270 --> 00:42:51.710
This seems to me somehow
related to the difficulties

00:42:51.710 --> 00:42:54.590
and the challenges that
Antonio was just talking about.

00:42:54.590 --> 00:42:57.080
But I wonder if you could
talk to us a little bit more

00:42:57.080 --> 00:42:59.420
about how you begin
to close that gap.

00:43:02.790 --> 00:43:05.910
Well, it became
really clear early on

00:43:05.910 --> 00:43:09.510
that I wanted to remain in the
driver's seat as a composer,

00:43:09.510 --> 00:43:11.320
so not to try to
outsource that job.

00:43:11.320 --> 00:43:13.710
And I think that speaks
directly to some of your earlier

00:43:13.710 --> 00:43:16.500
questions about
the kind of state

00:43:16.500 --> 00:43:18.540
that AI finds itself
in at the moment.

00:43:18.540 --> 00:43:22.590
I don't see it as
outperforming me

00:43:22.590 --> 00:43:26.310
in that role of contextualizing
the world around me

00:43:26.310 --> 00:43:29.820
and creating original works
that speak to my context

00:43:29.820 --> 00:43:31.080
specifically.

00:43:31.080 --> 00:43:34.410
So I did decide to kind of try
to focus on live performance.

00:43:34.410 --> 00:43:36.030
But of course this
is a huge challenge

00:43:36.030 --> 00:43:39.390
because with sound
and processing,

00:43:39.390 --> 00:43:41.028
you have a kind of latency.

00:43:41.028 --> 00:43:43.320
And so if you're trying to
perform, if you're singing--

00:43:43.320 --> 00:43:47.490
right now, there's a latency
between my camera and speaking

00:43:47.490 --> 00:43:51.690
to you, and it's already
slightly disorienting.

00:43:51.690 --> 00:43:55.470
But if it was happening
in audio on a stage,

00:43:55.470 --> 00:43:58.770
it would be almost kind of like
an insurmountable challenge.

00:43:58.770 --> 00:44:04.350
So the real problem is basically
computation time and, yeah,

00:44:04.350 --> 00:44:08.130
just the amount of
time before the signal

00:44:08.130 --> 00:44:10.493
can be processed before it
comes out the other end.

00:44:10.493 --> 00:44:12.660
So that's what we're basically
working on right now,

00:44:12.660 --> 00:44:16.710
trying to pre-train models that
we can then perform through

00:44:16.710 --> 00:44:18.190
that I find really interesting.

00:44:18.190 --> 00:44:23.100
But another way that we tried to
incorporate some of these ideas

00:44:23.100 --> 00:44:26.310
into our live performance before
we had the technology really

00:44:26.310 --> 00:44:32.580
ready is we were
looking at basically how

00:44:32.580 --> 00:44:34.620
as soon as anything
can become captured

00:44:34.620 --> 00:44:37.440
in media, like an audio
file, video file, text

00:44:37.440 --> 00:44:40.140
file, whatever, it can become
part of a training canon.

00:44:40.140 --> 00:44:43.050
So almost as a kind of
public announcement,

00:44:43.050 --> 00:44:46.170
we started incorporating
training ceremonies

00:44:46.170 --> 00:44:47.790
into our live concerts.

00:44:47.790 --> 00:44:49.950
So one of my vocalists
would sing a line,

00:44:49.950 --> 00:44:51.540
and the audience
would sing back.

00:44:51.540 --> 00:44:53.070
And we would record this.

00:44:53.070 --> 00:44:55.320
And we have recordings
from concerts

00:44:55.320 --> 00:44:57.540
all over the world with
different audiences.

00:44:57.540 --> 00:45:00.450
And we can then
create audience models

00:45:00.450 --> 00:45:03.480
that sound unique to each
room that was performing

00:45:03.480 --> 00:45:06.090
because different
countries, different cities

00:45:06.090 --> 00:45:08.820
have different vocal traditions,
different size crowds,

00:45:08.820 --> 00:45:10.330
et cetera.

00:45:10.330 --> 00:45:14.130
And so as a performer,
trying to find ways

00:45:14.130 --> 00:45:18.120
to communicate some of
the more complicated ideas

00:45:18.120 --> 00:45:20.880
to a wider public
through incorporating

00:45:20.880 --> 00:45:22.800
the public into a
training ceremony,

00:45:22.800 --> 00:45:25.050
but also trying to
asceticize the event

00:45:25.050 --> 00:45:30.390
and show that it does actually
require a kind of human labor

00:45:30.390 --> 00:45:34.320
and human expression in order
to create these training

00:45:34.320 --> 00:45:38.640
sets themselves as a way to
try to communicate that what

00:45:38.640 --> 00:45:40.560
comes out the other
end isn't just

00:45:40.560 --> 00:45:47.130
some kind of random
alien-produced thing.

00:45:47.130 --> 00:45:49.530
It's something that
comes from human culture

00:45:49.530 --> 00:45:51.870
and human creation.

00:45:51.870 --> 00:45:54.150
So, yeah, that's why
I've focused quite a bit

00:45:54.150 --> 00:45:57.090
on where my training
sets have come from,

00:45:57.090 --> 00:46:01.740
a lot about how we deal with
our shared human archive.

00:46:01.740 --> 00:46:04.350
It's one of the things I really
enjoyed about Markus's talk is

00:46:04.350 --> 00:46:07.500
that he's really thinking about
things on a really long time

00:46:07.500 --> 00:46:08.010
horizon.

00:46:08.010 --> 00:46:09.630
I've really been
thinking about it

00:46:09.630 --> 00:46:12.580
on a kind of time horizon
of recorded media.

00:46:12.580 --> 00:46:17.160
So we have this shared human
archive of video and film

00:46:17.160 --> 00:46:21.390
and photograph, and we
can reanimate the dead

00:46:21.390 --> 00:46:25.380
that we have an archive of
to kind of do whatever we

00:46:25.380 --> 00:46:26.948
want them to do in the future.

00:46:26.948 --> 00:46:29.490
And so I think it opens up all
kinds of interesting questions

00:46:29.490 --> 00:46:32.190
about what we do with
that shared human archive.

00:46:40.410 --> 00:46:42.810
So Holly, one question then.

00:46:42.810 --> 00:46:48.300
When you use these recordings--

00:46:48.300 --> 00:46:51.990
coming back to this
question of representations,

00:46:51.990 --> 00:46:55.630
you want to be on the driver's
seat of the AI system.

00:46:55.630 --> 00:46:59.850
So that means the AI
system is composing

00:46:59.850 --> 00:47:03.270
the sound in some ways,
building some representation

00:47:03.270 --> 00:47:06.330
that you can manipulate and
generate new sounds with.

00:47:06.330 --> 00:47:09.690
And my question when I
was watching your talk

00:47:09.690 --> 00:47:14.827
was, what type of
representations

00:47:14.827 --> 00:47:16.660
or what type of
compositions are you looking

00:47:16.660 --> 00:47:21.777
for so that you can actually
control them in the way

00:47:21.777 --> 00:47:22.360
that you want?

00:47:26.230 --> 00:47:29.610
So I have largely been focusing
on performance techniques.

00:47:29.610 --> 00:47:32.880
So when I say I want to
remain in the driver's seat,

00:47:32.880 --> 00:47:37.020
I'm largely writing the
phrases or the compositions,

00:47:37.020 --> 00:47:41.100
and then I'm performing
them with my human voice,

00:47:41.100 --> 00:47:43.200
but I'm able to
perform as a flute

00:47:43.200 --> 00:47:48.720
or as whatever kind of
instrument that I've modeled.

00:47:48.720 --> 00:47:50.100
So that's one way.

00:47:50.100 --> 00:47:54.450
Or another way is sometimes I'll
write something and then create

00:47:54.450 --> 00:48:01.350
variations on that motif
and then interject and pick

00:48:01.350 --> 00:48:04.800
the parts that are useful and
interesting and kind of move

00:48:04.800 --> 00:48:05.860
on from there.

00:48:05.860 --> 00:48:09.000
I think a lot of people who
are coming to music and machine

00:48:09.000 --> 00:48:11.370
learning that have maybe
less experience with it,

00:48:11.370 --> 00:48:18.410
they imagine this kind of fully
automated black box, where

00:48:18.410 --> 00:48:21.540
you just kind of type
in an idea and out comes

00:48:21.540 --> 00:48:23.840
this fully fleshed
piece of music.

00:48:23.840 --> 00:48:27.690
And maybe we will get
there, but two things.

00:48:27.690 --> 00:48:35.660
One thing is the information
that went to training in order

00:48:35.660 --> 00:48:38.480
to get the piece in
and of itself, that

00:48:38.480 --> 00:48:40.430
came from somewhere.

00:48:40.430 --> 00:48:43.820
That came from humans
musicing in space coming up

00:48:43.820 --> 00:48:45.267
with aesthetic ideas together.

00:48:45.267 --> 00:48:46.850
So that's one thing
that I don't think

00:48:46.850 --> 00:48:49.130
should be forgotten or erased.

00:48:49.130 --> 00:48:51.200
And the other thing is
that it's just simply not

00:48:51.200 --> 00:48:52.610
that sophisticated,
or at least what

00:48:52.610 --> 00:48:55.110
I've been working with is just
simply not that sophisticated

00:48:55.110 --> 00:48:56.030
yet.

00:48:56.030 --> 00:49:00.620
It has kind of equivalent of the
watermark all over the place.

00:49:00.620 --> 00:49:05.510
And it takes my composer's
ear to go in and remove

00:49:05.510 --> 00:49:10.070
the watermark or leave the
watermark, but with intention,

00:49:10.070 --> 00:49:13.190
and to make whatever
I'm creating

00:49:13.190 --> 00:49:15.170
have relevance to
the world around me.

00:49:15.170 --> 00:49:17.840
Because music isn't
just a combination

00:49:17.840 --> 00:49:19.630
of note relationships.

00:49:19.630 --> 00:49:23.150
It's not like we can just do
statistical analysis on scores

00:49:23.150 --> 00:49:28.880
and then fully understand the
human emotional aesthetic.

00:49:28.880 --> 00:49:32.630
It's constantly shifting.

00:49:32.630 --> 00:49:36.290
Whether or not an 808 drum
kit or a 707 drum kit,

00:49:36.290 --> 00:49:40.430
that brings with it an entire
history of electronic dance

00:49:40.430 --> 00:49:44.780
music to it that my
computer at this time

00:49:44.780 --> 00:49:46.520
doesn't understand
at all, doesn't

00:49:46.520 --> 00:49:48.950
understand that kind of
history or that kind of weight.

00:49:48.950 --> 00:49:54.080
So, yeah, I think while it is
so interesting and so powerful

00:49:54.080 --> 00:49:58.130
in so many ways, it is also so
limited and requires so much

00:49:58.130 --> 00:50:00.972
of my own intervention in order
to make something interesting.

00:50:00.972 --> 00:50:02.930
And that's why I think
it's exciting, actually.

00:50:05.960 --> 00:50:07.490
Can I add another part in?

00:50:07.490 --> 00:50:10.130
This is really great, Holly.

00:50:10.130 --> 00:50:12.650
One other thing that we
have found if we are maybe

00:50:12.650 --> 00:50:16.730
forgetting the AI component, but
just to look to natural systems

00:50:16.730 --> 00:50:19.190
like a protein or
molecules or a spider web,

00:50:19.190 --> 00:50:22.040
there are really interesting
musical structures in these.

00:50:22.040 --> 00:50:25.130
And so some of the work we
have not with the use of AI,

00:50:25.130 --> 00:50:28.160
but basically just
mirroring these structures

00:50:28.160 --> 00:50:32.390
into audible sound or musical
space, with human choices,

00:50:32.390 --> 00:50:33.120
of course.

00:50:33.120 --> 00:50:34.400
They have to be made.

00:50:34.400 --> 00:50:36.680
But there are a lot of
really interesting things

00:50:36.680 --> 00:50:39.020
not using AI, but just
looking to nature.

00:50:39.020 --> 00:50:42.348
And you can look at the
example of work on spider webs,

00:50:42.348 --> 00:50:44.390
where you look at the
really intricate sounds you

00:50:44.390 --> 00:50:47.450
can get as you wander around,
and the proteins, the folding,

00:50:47.450 --> 00:50:50.600
creating quite complex
counterpoint structures

00:50:50.600 --> 00:50:53.090
that are, however, not
necessarily the kinds of things

00:50:53.090 --> 00:50:53.820
that we expect.

00:50:53.820 --> 00:50:55.130
So it really comes
down to the frame.

00:50:55.130 --> 00:50:56.672
As you were talking,
I kept thinking,

00:50:56.672 --> 00:51:01.520
there is the moment of
expectation or the history.

00:51:01.520 --> 00:51:03.650
You mentioned the great
examples of the choices

00:51:03.650 --> 00:51:05.000
of the instrumentation.

00:51:05.000 --> 00:51:08.520
And the same goes for the music
generated by natural systems.

00:51:08.520 --> 00:51:10.370
And I think connects
also with what Gary was

00:51:10.370 --> 00:51:11.780
talking about with birdsong.

00:51:11.780 --> 00:51:13.670
These are not things
that we expect.

00:51:13.670 --> 00:51:15.320
And I think one
future evolution,

00:51:15.320 --> 00:51:18.620
I think as we become
part of the performance

00:51:18.620 --> 00:51:20.720
and the observation,
as you are doing,

00:51:20.720 --> 00:51:23.300
Holly, in your
performances, we actually

00:51:23.300 --> 00:51:25.520
become part of the
experiment as listeners.

00:51:25.520 --> 00:51:28.460
And I think the plasticity
that the species have,

00:51:28.460 --> 00:51:30.770
I think we have to maybe
develop that as listeners

00:51:30.770 --> 00:51:31.730
to music as well.

00:51:31.730 --> 00:51:34.610
And that opens up a whole new
dimension in understanding.

00:51:34.610 --> 00:51:37.790
And I think in the
world of people thinking

00:51:37.790 --> 00:51:39.770
about composition and
how music is evolving,

00:51:39.770 --> 00:51:41.660
that's more
prevalent, of course.

00:51:41.660 --> 00:51:45.470
But if you look at a
broader public, an audience,

00:51:45.470 --> 00:51:47.570
there's much room
to grow, I think.

00:51:47.570 --> 00:51:50.360
And I think that's a great
opportunity for all of us

00:51:50.360 --> 00:51:51.860
to work on and communicate.

00:51:51.860 --> 00:51:54.193
And that's why I was talking
earlier about the reference

00:51:54.193 --> 00:51:54.750
frame.

00:51:54.750 --> 00:51:57.770
If we begin with this weird
reference frame that nobody

00:51:57.770 --> 00:52:00.670
understands, we cannot bring
the audience to that point.

00:52:00.670 --> 00:52:03.018
So I oftentimes like
to move the audience

00:52:03.018 --> 00:52:04.310
from something they understand.

00:52:04.310 --> 00:52:06.620
And so the aria was
an example for that

00:52:06.620 --> 00:52:09.440
and moving away from that,
interpreting away from that,

00:52:09.440 --> 00:52:12.170
and then showing this
other space that opens up.

00:52:12.170 --> 00:52:14.000
But I feel also
as a teacher, you

00:52:14.000 --> 00:52:15.830
have to bring the
audience from--

00:52:15.830 --> 00:52:17.648
when I teach a
class at MIT, I have

00:52:17.648 --> 00:52:19.190
to bring the students
from where they

00:52:19.190 --> 00:52:22.185
are to where I want them to
be or what I want them to see.

00:52:22.185 --> 00:52:23.810
And I think similarly
in music as well.

00:52:31.620 --> 00:52:34.020
Antonio, I'm curious.

00:52:34.020 --> 00:52:39.930
When you hear Holly speaking
about these ritualized

00:52:39.930 --> 00:52:46.860
entrainments, this reminds me
of that ingenious and hilarious

00:52:46.860 --> 00:52:50.340
passage from your presentation
with the greatest hits data

00:52:50.340 --> 00:52:52.440
set, where there
was almost some kind

00:52:52.440 --> 00:52:57.690
of theatrical presentation of
the process of producing data.

00:52:57.690 --> 00:53:00.660
And this was another
theme that I also

00:53:00.660 --> 00:53:02.910
hear Markus talking
about regularly,

00:53:02.910 --> 00:53:07.080
about how much of the
creativity is going on in AI

00:53:07.080 --> 00:53:10.130
and the arts has to do with
the generation of data sets.

00:53:10.130 --> 00:53:16.290
So when you hear these
thoughts about the plasticity

00:53:16.290 --> 00:53:19.530
of a public's perception
of a musical tradition

00:53:19.530 --> 00:53:22.170
being the medium
that's being worked

00:53:22.170 --> 00:53:27.330
upon through the practice
of training and AI,

00:53:27.330 --> 00:53:31.320
does this incite
your imagination

00:53:31.320 --> 00:53:35.760
as a practitioner within the
bowels of the Gary building

00:53:35.760 --> 00:53:38.460
over at CSAIL?

00:53:38.460 --> 00:53:43.140
So I think that the creation
of the training data

00:53:43.140 --> 00:53:46.770
sometimes is a
little bit considered

00:53:46.770 --> 00:53:50.430
like a secondary job.

00:53:50.430 --> 00:53:53.500
That is, at least in
technical settings.

00:53:53.500 --> 00:53:56.130
It's always a lot more
interesting to develop

00:53:56.130 --> 00:53:59.790
the algorithms, and the
training sets themselves are not

00:53:59.790 --> 00:54:01.290
so interesting.

00:54:01.290 --> 00:54:02.790
I actually think that--

00:54:02.790 --> 00:54:04.650
well, not just me.

00:54:04.650 --> 00:54:08.580
Many people do-- but training
sets are actually just as

00:54:08.580 --> 00:54:09.780
important as the algorithm.

00:54:09.780 --> 00:54:11.970
They are the question
that you want to answer.

00:54:11.970 --> 00:54:15.360
And the creativity that goes
into producing the data set

00:54:15.360 --> 00:54:18.480
sometimes is just way more
important than the algorithm

00:54:18.480 --> 00:54:19.970
itself.

00:54:19.970 --> 00:54:22.410
For instance, what
Holly was mentioning

00:54:22.410 --> 00:54:27.360
about recording different
audiences around the world,

00:54:27.360 --> 00:54:30.330
that type of data is
really interesting.

00:54:30.330 --> 00:54:34.560
And I'm sure it will create lots
of opportunities of producing

00:54:34.560 --> 00:54:36.210
some pretty creative results.

00:54:36.210 --> 00:54:39.465
The algorithm itself is
going to be secondary.

00:54:39.465 --> 00:54:41.430
It certainly will
change the outcome,

00:54:41.430 --> 00:54:44.100
but it's going to be
kind of secondary.

00:54:44.100 --> 00:54:47.940
The main thing is the data
that was used to train.

00:54:47.940 --> 00:54:51.390
And I think that thinking
about the training

00:54:51.390 --> 00:54:54.060
sets, the questions
that you want to ask

00:54:54.060 --> 00:54:59.400
and how that training
points towards an answer.

00:54:59.400 --> 00:55:04.050
How do you avoid maybe putting
too much of your biases

00:55:04.050 --> 00:55:06.520
towards what you think
the answers should be

00:55:06.520 --> 00:55:08.320
into how you collect the data?

00:55:08.320 --> 00:55:10.510
I think that those things
are really important.

00:55:10.510 --> 00:55:15.510
And I think that, as
you were mentioning,

00:55:15.510 --> 00:55:17.790
the training data
set is probably

00:55:17.790 --> 00:55:20.790
one of the most important
parts of the creative process.

00:55:24.200 --> 00:55:26.840
Can I add something
to that really quick?

00:55:26.840 --> 00:55:30.980
So this is one of the reasons
why we like to use AI for data

00:55:30.980 --> 00:55:33.710
from proteins or
DNA or maybe sounds

00:55:33.710 --> 00:55:36.860
from spiders is because
if we filter them

00:55:36.860 --> 00:55:39.980
through our own brain and then
extract information, let's

00:55:39.980 --> 00:55:41.600
say for musical
inspiration, we're

00:55:41.600 --> 00:55:44.285
biased by the kind of
history we have in our brain.

00:55:44.285 --> 00:55:47.010
AI is kind of, for me--
and I think it was Holly.

00:55:47.010 --> 00:55:49.790
You mentioned this in
your talk, that you

00:55:49.790 --> 00:55:51.560
start from a blank
slate, essentially.

00:55:51.560 --> 00:55:53.030
The AI has never heard anything.

00:55:53.030 --> 00:55:54.930
It's never heard
any music at all.

00:55:54.930 --> 00:55:56.715
So the first music
that, in my case,

00:55:56.715 --> 00:55:57.840
I don't have a name for it.

00:55:57.840 --> 00:55:59.450
You have a great name for it.

00:55:59.450 --> 00:56:02.600
But our system,
our computer system

00:56:02.600 --> 00:56:05.270
listens to these hundreds
and thousands of proteins

00:56:05.270 --> 00:56:07.837
and has a very abstract
knowledge of these

00:56:07.837 --> 00:56:09.170
but never heard any other music.

00:56:09.170 --> 00:56:11.480
And so that sense,
it's a great way for us

00:56:11.480 --> 00:56:13.910
to start from a
clean slate and then

00:56:13.910 --> 00:56:16.130
incorporate the human
invention after that fact.

00:56:16.130 --> 00:56:18.740
So we found them to be extremely
useful and important to avoid

00:56:18.740 --> 00:56:20.780
the bias from the
human perception.

00:56:28.387 --> 00:56:29.970
Gary, were you going
to jump in there?

00:56:29.970 --> 00:56:32.790
I had a thought about your
work, actually, there.

00:56:32.790 --> 00:56:37.410
And I think we might actually
be edging towards an answer

00:56:37.410 --> 00:56:38.940
to your provocation.

00:56:38.940 --> 00:56:41.700
Because if we were to
think of the AI system

00:56:41.700 --> 00:56:44.820
itself as being
responsible for answering

00:56:44.820 --> 00:56:47.310
the question of the
generation of meaning,

00:56:47.310 --> 00:56:50.580
we might be missing out some
of these other observations

00:56:50.580 --> 00:56:55.140
about how that algorithm
as an analog for the gene

00:56:55.140 --> 00:56:57.420
is always within a context.

00:56:57.420 --> 00:56:59.850
And that context
has to be something

00:56:59.850 --> 00:57:06.480
that is transforming and
rendering plastic the movement

00:57:06.480 --> 00:57:08.340
of the genetic code for time.

00:57:08.340 --> 00:57:11.100
So you have to set in motion,
as you were saying, Gary.

00:57:11.100 --> 00:57:13.620
You have to set the
algorithm in motion

00:57:13.620 --> 00:57:15.960
through these acts
of contextualisation,

00:57:15.960 --> 00:57:19.890
through these acts of
carefully managing the creation

00:57:19.890 --> 00:57:22.890
and preservation of
an archive for the AI

00:57:22.890 --> 00:57:25.650
to genuinely gain this
radical plasticity

00:57:25.650 --> 00:57:26.940
of niche construction.

00:57:26.940 --> 00:57:30.690
And this is something that I
find so fascinating hearing

00:57:30.690 --> 00:57:33.870
about Markus and
Holly discussing

00:57:33.870 --> 00:57:35.640
these various musical archives.

00:57:35.640 --> 00:57:38.640
Because it does really
bring to the foreground

00:57:38.640 --> 00:57:41.940
how this entitlement to
shared cultural history

00:57:41.940 --> 00:57:44.310
is very much at stake
in these discussions.

00:57:44.310 --> 00:57:48.630
And that creating some
kind of ground around which

00:57:48.630 --> 00:57:52.260
to recognize the preservation
of these materials

00:57:52.260 --> 00:57:54.810
and this data and
the organization

00:57:54.810 --> 00:57:56.520
and structuring of
it is really what

00:57:56.520 --> 00:57:59.580
will set the algorithm in
motion and transform it

00:57:59.580 --> 00:58:03.450
into a process, to elevate
it above the emergence

00:58:03.450 --> 00:58:05.820
of the function or
of optimization.

00:58:05.820 --> 00:58:08.370
So these might still
be early beginnings,

00:58:08.370 --> 00:58:11.400
but I think we're hearing
in this discussion

00:58:11.400 --> 00:58:13.620
that the importance
of composition

00:58:13.620 --> 00:58:16.590
and of contextualization
can never

00:58:16.590 --> 00:58:20.430
be extricated from the design
of the algorithm, in that

00:58:20.430 --> 00:58:25.230
the algorithm is nothing without
the data set that feeds it,

00:58:25.230 --> 00:58:28.290
in other words.

00:58:28.290 --> 00:58:36.383
In that sense, when I
think about an embodied AI,

00:58:36.383 --> 00:58:38.550
I think these examples that
we've been hearing today

00:58:38.550 --> 00:58:42.540
are all instances
of embodiment in AI.

00:58:42.540 --> 00:58:45.330
It is not so much
about a body as it

00:58:45.330 --> 00:58:48.900
is about a set of contexts
within which algorithms

00:58:48.900 --> 00:58:49.620
are unfolding.

00:58:49.620 --> 00:58:51.090
And the data sets
have everything

00:58:51.090 --> 00:58:52.200
to do with those contexts.

00:58:52.200 --> 00:58:54.570
They are crucial, as Antonio
was just making clear,

00:58:54.570 --> 00:58:58.050
and as both Markus and Holly's
presentations made very, very

00:58:58.050 --> 00:59:00.330
clear.

00:59:00.330 --> 00:59:03.340
All of these are
reflecting human choices.

00:59:03.340 --> 00:59:06.150
I'm not one to think that we
are anywhere close to a Kurzweil

00:59:06.150 --> 00:59:10.900
singularity in terms of
AI taking over the world

00:59:10.900 --> 00:59:12.270
and so on.

00:59:12.270 --> 00:59:14.760
But what it brings home
to me again and again

00:59:14.760 --> 00:59:19.680
is the miraculous nature
of even the simplest life

00:59:19.680 --> 00:59:22.650
forms that we can think
of, the miraculous nature

00:59:22.650 --> 00:59:27.480
of the complexity of their
moment-to-moment embodiment

00:59:27.480 --> 00:59:31.140
and contextualization.

00:59:31.140 --> 00:59:33.270
And this, it seems to me--

00:59:33.270 --> 00:59:37.590
the challenge of AI for me as
an outsider to all of this,

00:59:37.590 --> 00:59:39.750
fascinated by it but
very much an outsider,

00:59:39.750 --> 00:59:42.630
the challenge is not to
create the machine that

00:59:42.630 --> 00:59:43.950
thinks like a human mind.

00:59:43.950 --> 00:59:49.040
The challenge is
to create systems

00:59:49.040 --> 00:59:53.450
that are interacting with their
environments that approximates

00:59:53.450 --> 00:59:58.610
some degree of complexity
for even the simplest life

00:59:58.610 --> 01:00:01.190
forms interacting with
their environments.

01:00:01.190 --> 01:00:02.930
Or so it seems to
me, but this is just

01:00:02.930 --> 01:00:06.910
the question that occupies me,
I guess, when it comes to AI.

01:00:06.910 --> 01:00:08.980
And I think it's a question
worth contemplating

01:00:08.980 --> 01:00:11.050
for many years to
come, certainly.

01:00:11.050 --> 01:00:15.020
And with that, we have reached
the end of our hour-long time

01:00:15.020 --> 01:00:15.520
slot.

01:00:15.520 --> 01:00:17.440
So I just wanted
to thank all of you

01:00:17.440 --> 01:00:18.970
for these brilliant comments.

01:00:18.970 --> 01:00:21.580
And I think we've made a
lot of connections here

01:00:21.580 --> 01:00:24.610
that were nascent in your talks.

01:00:24.610 --> 01:00:26.620
And I also want to
point out that we've

01:00:26.620 --> 01:00:30.880
hit upon a number of topics that
will be coming up explicitly

01:00:30.880 --> 01:00:33.310
as the focus of discussion
in the other panels.

01:00:33.310 --> 01:00:36.580
The question of bias
in AI will be addressed

01:00:36.580 --> 01:00:41.050
later on in the program,
as will genetic algorithms,

01:00:41.050 --> 01:00:43.150
and data set creation,
and open systems,

01:00:43.150 --> 01:00:45.610
and even later today,
unfolding models.

01:00:45.610 --> 01:00:49.240
So I invite you all to
join me in the continuation

01:00:49.240 --> 01:00:51.662
of these discussions
as we've reinforced

01:00:51.662 --> 01:00:53.620
the connections to these
other important topics

01:00:53.620 --> 01:00:54.650
in the discussion.

01:00:54.650 --> 01:00:57.850
And with that, I suppose
we will sign off.

01:00:57.850 --> 01:01:00.400
And I would like to thank
you all again so much

01:01:00.400 --> 01:01:04.160
for your contributions today.

01:01:04.160 --> 01:01:05.630
Thank you.

01:01:05.630 --> 01:01:07.420
Thank you.

